Part A: Postgres stores JSONB as a binary-parsed blob. To update a single key, Postgres must rewrite the entire column value for that row. Under concurrent writes, two transactions each read the current blob, modify the counter key in memory, and write back — the second write overwrites the first, losing one increment. A plain int column supports atomic UPDATE SET view_count = view_count + 1 which Postgres executes as a single in-place operation with row-level locking — no read-modify-write race is possible.

Part B (pseudocode):
up(): {
  this.addColumn('article', { name: 'view_count', type: 'int', default: 0, nullable: false });
  this.addSql("UPDATE article SET view_count = (metadata->>'readTimeMinutes')::int WHERE metadata->>'readTimeMinutes' IS NOT NULL");
  // Note: dropping a JSONB key requires updating the column, not dropping it entirely unless metadata only held the counter:
  // this.addSql("UPDATE article SET metadata = metadata - 'readTimeMinutes'");
}
Filter update: if the softDelete filter condition references metadata->'deletedAt', nothing changes. If viewCount was part of a filter condition (e.g. $gt: minViews), update the filter to reference the new column.
